1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:10,000
80,000 tons of floating steel from the first primitive flat tops to nuclear powered supercarriers.

2
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:16,000
They are unrivaled in their ability to deliver massive firepower to the enemy's doorstep.

3
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:22,000
Now, aircraft carriers on modern marvels, then and now.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
On August 2nd, 1990, when Iraq invaded Kuwait,

5
00:00:44,560 --> 00:00:51,300
the carrier USS Independence and her battle group were performing standard fleet exercises in the Indian Ocean.

6
00:00:51,301 --> 00:00:58,780
within hours they had moved into the gulf and were ready to support saudi arabian defense efforts in

7
00:00:58,781 --> 00:01:07,000
the air or at sea in those first few days the independence battle group represented the only

8
00:01:07,001 --> 00:01:14,020
u.s offensive strike power in the region when we have national crises like the crisis in iraq

9
00:01:14,021 --> 00:01:19,240
it's you saw the problem on getting forces there i mean ground troops you've got to fly in you've

10
00:01:19,241 --> 00:01:23,240
have supplies for them everything else they showed every country that they could be on the scene within

11
00:01:23,800 --> 00:01:28,360
a matter of hours from the from the mediterranean within 48 hours they're on the scene capable of

12
00:01:28,361 --> 00:01:33,160
laying down three squadrons of attack aircraft two squadrons of fighter aircraft day and night

13
00:01:33,161 --> 00:01:37,800
off of each aircraft carrier you have what you need almost no matter what you need

14
00:01:38,600 --> 00:01:44,760
at almost any point in the globe desert storm showed that the maybe could operate at the same

15
00:01:44,761 --> 00:01:49,880
sortie rates as the Air Force, plane type by plane type.

16
00:01:50,920 --> 00:01:57,019
By the time Operation Desert Storm began on January 17, 1991, the U.S.

17
00:01:57,020 --> 00:01:58,500
Navy had deployed

18
00:01:58,501 --> 00:02:05,640
six aircraft carrier groups to the Persian Gulf and the Red Sea, capable of launching

19
00:02:05,641 --> 00:02:10,240
over 300 of the world's most advanced strike and interceptor aircraft.

20
00:02:10,241 --> 00:02:21,420
The Navy's F-14s, A-6Es, F-A-18s and A-7s flew thousands of strikes against targets in Iraq and Kuwait

21
00:02:21,421 --> 00:02:26,120
and dropped over 11,000 tons of ordnance.

22
00:02:29,700 --> 00:02:37,240
These aircraft carried out attack, reconnaissance and fighter missions just like their land-based brethren.

23
00:02:37,241 --> 00:02:42,240
They were perfectly capable of operating from land or from sea.

24
00:02:42,241 --> 00:02:45,240
We're talking about a miniature air force.

25
00:02:45,241 --> 00:02:45,941
One U.S.

26
00:02:45,942 --> 00:02:51,240
carrier's air complement is the equivalent to most of the nations in the world's air forces.

27
00:02:51,241 --> 00:02:55,240
Our missions tend to be very long, four and a half to five hours.

28
00:02:55,241 --> 00:02:59,240
Brief normally takes place about three hours prior to takeoff.

29
00:02:59,241 --> 00:03:03,240
Man your airplane up an hour prior to your launch time.

30
00:03:03,241 --> 00:03:13,440
Once we take off, we end up going feet dry, proceeding about 380 miles over a desert where you have no reference, no towns, no tack hands.

31
00:03:14,100 --> 00:03:17,560
So you're basically trusting the systems in your airplane, your own gut feeling.

32
00:03:17,940 --> 00:03:24,020
The F-18 provides us a unique opportunity to fill several roles.

33
00:03:25,360 --> 00:03:32,060
We are primarily carrying ordnance across into Iraq to military targets.

34
00:03:32,061 --> 00:03:36,260
The bomb damage assessment that we've brought back from our strikes

35
00:03:36,261 --> 00:03:40,260
indicate that the airplane is truly accurate from virtually any altitude.

36
00:03:41,180 --> 00:03:45,520
Its agility and its computer systems just make it a dream in combat.

37
00:03:46,400 --> 00:03:52,640
The multi-billion dollar aircraft carrier accomplished its mission in the Gulf with remarkable precision.

38
00:03:53,440 --> 00:03:58,400
The carrier's role as a highly mobile, self-reliant and heavily armed military force

39
00:03:58,401 --> 00:04:00,560
made its presence invaluable.

40
00:04:02,060 --> 00:04:07,640
It's a very, very effective weapon, a mobile airfield that can go anywhere in the world.

41
00:04:07,880 --> 00:04:15,220
And there's basically very few strategic targets that an aircraft carrier cannot hit with its conventional or nuclear capability.

42
00:04:15,620 --> 00:04:16,320
None.

43
00:04:16,440 --> 00:04:19,960
It could have been one, I don't believe, without the aid of the aircraft carriers.

44
00:04:20,920 --> 00:04:28,220
The evolution of these technological marvels began 90 years earlier when the dream of flight became a reality.

45
00:04:29,140 --> 00:04:34,460
The Wright brothers' first powered flight at Kitty Hawk in 1903 had scarcely touched down

46
00:04:34,461 --> 00:04:39,340
when some military experts began to view the airplane as a war machine.

47
00:04:40,400 --> 00:04:43,840
Not only could it serve as a land-based weapon, they reasoned,

48
00:04:43,960 --> 00:04:46,600
it could also be operated from a ship at sea.

49
00:04:47,460 --> 00:04:49,500
But naval officials had their doubts.

50
00:04:49,740 --> 00:04:51,809
In September of 1910, the U.S.

51
00:04:51,810 --> 00:04:56,980
Navy gave the okay to launch an aircraft from one of their battle cruisers,

52
00:04:56,981 --> 00:05:04,380
spurred both by curiosity and the fear that a German steamship line was about to attempt the same experiment.

53
00:05:05,300 --> 00:05:08,680
The ship was easy to find, but the pilot was not.

54
00:05:09,420 --> 00:05:11,620
Wilbur Wright rejected the Navy's offer,

55
00:05:11,621 --> 00:05:15,900
but fortunately a visionary American aviator named Glenn Curtis

56
00:05:15,901 --> 00:05:20,240
and a stunt pilot named Eugene Eli were eager to assist.

57
00:05:20,241 --> 00:05:29,660
In November of 1910, Eugene Eli raced his Curtis Pusher Model D biplane off the forecastle of the USS Birmingham.

58
00:05:30,640 --> 00:05:32,100
The flight was a success.

59
00:05:32,920 --> 00:05:36,720
Several months later, they were ready to attempt a landing on a cruiser.

60
00:05:37,640 --> 00:05:43,060
A hook was mounted underneath the plane to engage wires weighted only with sandbags.

61
00:05:43,800 --> 00:05:46,540
This primitive arrestor system proved successful.

62
00:05:47,320 --> 00:05:49,220
With the success of these experiments,

63
00:05:49,720 --> 00:05:54,140
the Navy was now guardedly optimistic about the future of naval aviation.

64
00:05:57,600 --> 00:06:00,620
I got the idea that I wanted to learn to fly,

65
00:06:00,820 --> 00:06:05,800
that naval aviation would amount to something for naval purposes.

66
00:06:06,800 --> 00:06:08,900
So I put in a request to the Navy Department,

67
00:06:09,260 --> 00:06:11,900
and they came back and quite frankly said

68
00:06:11,901 --> 00:06:15,660
that they didn't believe aviation would ever amount to anything.

69
00:06:16,540 --> 00:06:20,760
But if it turned out to be otherwise, they would consider my request.

70
00:06:20,960 --> 00:06:26,840
During that winter, Congress appropriated money for the Navy to buy three airplanes.

71
00:06:29,140 --> 00:06:30,433
In 1914, the U.S.

72
00:06:30,434 --> 00:06:35,680
Navy founded the Naval Aviation Flight Academy at Pensacola, Florida.

73
00:06:36,500 --> 00:06:41,060
Almost immediately, young men volunteered to become pioneers of naval aviation.

74
00:06:43,340 --> 00:06:46,200
During this period, Britain ruled the seas.

75
00:06:46,540 --> 00:06:51,700
Always open to new ideas, the British also began developing naval aviation.

76
00:06:55,360 --> 00:07:00,020
It was really the First World War which spurred the Royal Navy into putting aircraft to sea.

77
00:07:00,420 --> 00:07:04,240
The major reason for putting aircraft on ships was to extend the eyes of the Admiral

78
00:07:04,241 --> 00:07:07,640
so he could see over the horizon and spot for enemy battle fleets.

79
00:07:08,120 --> 00:07:13,740
So in the early days of the First War, the Royal Navy took over a number of cross-channel steamers,

80
00:07:14,360 --> 00:07:15,960
converted them to carry seaplanes.

81
00:07:16,540 --> 00:07:26,440
This early adaptation of a modern aircraft carrier took modified planes that would land in the water alongside a fighting ship and then were hoisted on board.

82
00:07:27,120 --> 00:07:30,780
The United States also used seaplanes in World War I.

83
00:07:31,600 --> 00:07:36,040
Both nations saw seaplanes as a possible means to attack German U-boats.

84
00:07:36,800 --> 00:07:39,960
But it was soon found that seaplanes had limited capabilities.

85
00:07:40,800 --> 00:07:43,540
They could only be launched and recovered in calm weather.

86
00:07:43,541 --> 00:07:47,420
When storms prevailed, seaplanes were rendered useless.

87
00:07:48,660 --> 00:07:54,700
Soon, Britain and the United States developed catapults which more easily launched aircraft from ships.

88
00:07:55,560 --> 00:08:03,360
With these innovations, both countries were now ready to create ships devoted solely to launching and landing aircraft.

89
00:08:05,080 --> 00:08:11,560
The first aircraft carriers, in the more traditional sense, were not special built.

90
00:08:11,561 --> 00:08:14,280
They were converted from other vessels, armored cruisers or something,

91
00:08:14,780 --> 00:08:18,260
and they featured relatively short decks and primitive landing systems.

92
00:08:19,940 --> 00:08:24,260
The British converted an Italian passenger liner into a carrier

93
00:08:24,261 --> 00:08:28,260
and named it the HMS Argus in September of 1918.

94
00:08:29,020 --> 00:08:34,360
It was truly a flat top with no masts, no stacks, and no superstructure.

95
00:08:35,800 --> 00:08:39,560
The navigation deck was constructed flush with the main deck,

96
00:08:39,561 --> 00:08:40,980
providing a smooth runway.

97
00:08:41,560 --> 00:08:46,940
The Americans were soon to follow with the USS Langley, a converted coal ship.

98
00:08:47,840 --> 00:08:51,320
The Langley's flight deck measured 542 feet in length,

99
00:08:51,760 --> 00:08:54,720
and like the Argus, was completely free of any obstruction.

100
00:09:00,480 --> 00:09:04,140
The carrier could hold 30 flight-ready aircraft,

101
00:09:04,141 --> 00:09:10,620
and an elevator was installed to transport the planes back and forth from the flight deck to the hangar below.

102
00:09:11,560 --> 00:09:14,300
the Navy quickly began testing their new invention.

103
00:09:15,320 --> 00:09:17,600
Early attempts at landing were highly dangerous.

104
00:09:18,540 --> 00:09:21,940
The light aircraft were likely to be caught by gusts of wind

105
00:09:21,941 --> 00:09:25,600
and either crash on the deck or swerve over the side.

106
00:09:26,820 --> 00:09:31,800
Both the British and Americans experimented with systems of longitudinal wires

107
00:09:31,801 --> 00:09:36,680
along the length of the flight deck to steady the aircraft until it slowed down.

108
00:09:41,560 --> 00:09:51,280
This system was not effective, so engineers devised an improved horizontal wire system with wires connected to weights under the deck.

109
00:09:51,980 --> 00:09:58,180
With these early technical innovations, the aircraft carrier became a viable weapon of war.

110
00:09:58,181 --> 00:10:08,860
1999 is on record as the safest year for the United States Navy

111
00:10:08,861 --> 00:10:13,500
setting a new record in total aviation operational and shore mishaps.

112
00:10:14,060 --> 00:10:18,640
Aircraft carriers will continue in a moment on Modern Marvel's Then and Now.

113
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:26,380
In order to limit the large number of warships in the post-war era,

114
00:10:26,920 --> 00:10:32,920
naval leaders from around the world gathered to draft the Washington Naval Disarmament Treaty of 1921.

115
00:10:33,840 --> 00:10:37,700
The treaty provided the first formal definition of an aircraft carrier,

116
00:10:38,360 --> 00:10:44,600
a warship with a water displacement in excess of 10,000 tons but not more than 27,000 tons,

117
00:10:45,160 --> 00:10:50,460
designed for the specific and exclusive purpose of carrying, launching, and landing aircraft.

118
00:10:51,180 --> 00:10:56,300
Under the new treaty restrictions, the U.S. Navy was able to build two more carriers

119
00:10:56,301 --> 00:10:58,580
from existing battlecruiser hulls.

120
00:10:59,340 --> 00:11:01,720
The converted carriers Lexington and Saratoga

121
00:11:01,721 --> 00:11:05,320
had flight decks that were substantially longer than the Langley

122
00:11:05,321 --> 00:11:07,620
and they could carry twice as many planes.

123
00:11:08,820 --> 00:11:11,120
The Navy chose an open-hanger design

124
00:11:11,121 --> 00:11:14,320
which allowed the maximum number of aircraft to be carried

125
00:11:14,321 --> 00:11:17,680
and provided for better ventilation of gasoline fumes

126
00:11:17,681 --> 00:11:20,080
while aircraft engines were being warmed up.

127
00:11:20,700 --> 00:11:23,040
The Japanese were quick to complete

128
00:11:23,041 --> 00:11:26,400
the world's first true aircraft carrier, the Hoshow.

129
00:11:27,140 --> 00:11:30,880
It was a small ship capable of carrying only 26 aircraft.

130
00:11:32,160 --> 00:11:37,200
It wasn't until 1934 that the United States commissioned its first true carrier,

131
00:11:37,600 --> 00:11:38,680
the USS Ranger.

132
00:11:39,580 --> 00:11:42,460
This ship was substantially smaller than its predecessors,

133
00:11:42,461 --> 00:11:45,340
but could hold almost the same amount of aircraft.

134
00:11:46,960 --> 00:11:50,800
These carriers incorporated the first hydraulic arrestor gear system.

135
00:11:50,801 --> 00:11:58,020
If a plane began to swerve to one side, a compensating mechanism would increase pressure and tug the plane to the center of the deck.

136
00:11:58,620 --> 00:12:02,080
This system, modified and improved, is still in use today.

137
00:12:02,400 --> 00:12:05,300
I first qualified in a fighter back in February 36.

138
00:12:06,320 --> 00:12:12,120
I found it quite exciting because it looked like a fairly small landing area.

139
00:12:12,540 --> 00:12:18,180
I had some trepidation about flying an airplane in cooperation with somebody else.

140
00:12:18,181 --> 00:12:20,340
And that somebody else would be the landing signals officer.

141
00:12:20,800 --> 00:12:25,340
We used to do six or nine landings before we were qualified,

142
00:12:25,640 --> 00:12:27,200
at least before we were registered as qualified.

143
00:12:27,360 --> 00:12:29,620
We obviously were qualified by the time we'd done the second one.

144
00:12:30,800 --> 00:12:34,260
The Yorktown Enterprise Hornet and Wasp followed.

145
00:12:35,380 --> 00:12:39,960
These carriers were faster, larger, and more heavily armored than their predecessors.

146
00:12:40,760 --> 00:12:45,760
They had to be to carry the more advanced aircraft that were quickly being developed.

147
00:12:45,761 --> 00:12:52,120
the requirements for carrier aircraft were very different than land-based aircraft they had to be

148
00:12:52,121 --> 00:12:58,140
very rugged they had to use radial engines as a rule because those were easier to maintain and

149
00:12:58,141 --> 00:13:02,380
service on a carrier and more than anything else the pilots had to be highly trained because they

150
00:13:02,381 --> 00:13:08,720
had to find a moving target out at sea between the wars britain japan and the u.s perfected

151
00:13:08,721 --> 00:13:14,560
almost every technique needed for successfully launching and operating aircraft from a carrier

152
00:13:14,561 --> 00:13:21,920
but the carrier was still considered mainly a scout in the battle fleets

153
00:13:21,921 --> 00:13:26,320
its potential as an attack vehicle had not yet been realized

154
00:13:26,321 --> 00:13:34,240
the carrier was really nothing much more than a landing and takeoff field for the airplanes

155
00:13:34,241 --> 00:13:36,460
it had no other function except to carry airplanes

156
00:13:36,461 --> 00:13:40,060
but by the time the war star the realization had developed

157
00:13:40,061 --> 00:13:43,700
that the carrier was an attack weapon and not a scouting weapon

158
00:13:43,701 --> 00:13:50,900
It would be the Japanese who would be the first to establish the aircraft carrier in a powerful new role.

159
00:14:00,180 --> 00:14:02,680
December 7th, 1941.

160
00:14:03,520 --> 00:14:09,340
The Japanese launched a strike force of 350 aircraft from six aircraft carriers,

161
00:14:09,341 --> 00:14:12,660
more than 300 miles off the coast of Hawaii.

162
00:14:13,700 --> 00:14:27,700
In two separate strikes, superbly trained air crews severely crippled or destroyed all but one of the mighty American battleships.

163
00:14:32,680 --> 00:14:36,460
Hundreds of American planes on the ground were lost.

164
00:14:37,340 --> 00:14:41,400
The Army and Navy suffered more than 4,500 casualties.

165
00:14:41,401 --> 00:14:46,280
Japanese carriers had inflicted a defeat upon the United States at Pearl Harbor

166
00:14:46,281 --> 00:14:49,500
but they didn't get the American carriers that were there

167
00:14:49,501 --> 00:14:53,420
Although the harbor seemed in total chaos

168
00:14:53,421 --> 00:14:57,540
the attack had not been the decisive victory the Japanese had planned

169
00:14:57,541 --> 00:15:03,100
Japanese intelligence had failed to locate the carriers of the Pacific Fleet

170
00:15:03,101 --> 00:15:04,660
normally based at Pearl

171
00:15:04,661 --> 00:15:09,260
The Japanese had made the carrier the most important ship in their navy

172
00:15:09,261 --> 00:15:14,160
and now the Americans would be forced to make it the centerpiece of their fleet as well.

173
00:15:15,560 --> 00:15:18,520
The Pacific War had become the carrier war.

174
00:15:19,140 --> 00:15:23,680
Within hours, the United States was an active participant in World War II.

175
00:15:25,220 --> 00:15:28,940
We will gain the inevitable triumph.

176
00:15:29,660 --> 00:15:31,000
So help us God.

177
00:15:31,900 --> 00:15:37,200
But in the early stages of the Pacific War, the Japanese enjoyed most of the victories.

178
00:15:38,120 --> 00:15:41,140
American forces desperately needed a boost in morale.

179
00:15:42,400 --> 00:15:50,840
In April of 1942, 16 Army B-25 bombers set sail on the carrier USS Hornet bound for Tokyo.

180
00:15:51,240 --> 00:15:57,280
The Americans were hoping to divert some of the Japanese military force away from their South Sea objective,

181
00:15:57,860 --> 00:16:01,360
as well as prove they were capable of striking the Japanese homeland.

182
00:16:01,361 --> 00:16:09,600
The raid, led by Lieutenant Colonel James Doolittle, began when the carrier was still

183
00:16:09,601 --> 00:16:11,640
hundreds of miles away from the target.

184
00:16:14,740 --> 00:16:16,920
Doolittle's own plane led the assault.

185
00:16:17,720 --> 00:16:21,700
It took a full hour to lift all the heavy bombers from the carrier deck.

186
00:16:31,360 --> 00:16:35,220
The raid was a public relations success for the U.S. government

187
00:16:35,221 --> 00:16:40,280
because it proved to the American people that the Japanese Empire was not invincible.

188
00:16:41,760 --> 00:16:46,620
The first great carrier versus carrier battle occurred in the Coral Sea,

189
00:16:47,100 --> 00:16:52,120
the first time in naval history the opposing ships never came in sight of each other.

190
00:16:52,620 --> 00:16:57,560
The aircraft were taking the battle away from the direct conflict between one warship and another,

191
00:16:57,960 --> 00:17:01,260
the sort of engagements between battleships that were seen in the First World War.

192
00:17:01,360 --> 00:17:03,600
and they were fighting the war at a distance.

193
00:17:04,700 --> 00:17:07,840
The Battle of Midway was fought the following month.

194
00:17:08,380 --> 00:17:10,580
To the great advantage of the United States,

195
00:17:10,840 --> 00:17:14,360
intelligence was able to decode the Japanese attack plans.

196
00:17:14,840 --> 00:17:18,100
On the morning of the 4th, shortly after daybreak,

197
00:17:18,700 --> 00:17:22,820
the first scouting patrol planes picked up the Japanese fleet

198
00:17:22,821 --> 00:17:25,240
about 230 miles off Midway.

199
00:17:25,720 --> 00:17:27,080
It seemed too good to be true.

200
00:17:27,300 --> 00:17:29,000
So they started launching right away.

201
00:17:29,001 --> 00:17:34,380
I put my two wing divisions out on either side,

202
00:17:34,560 --> 00:17:37,740
and I headed straight for the nearest target, which was the Akagi.

203
00:17:38,360 --> 00:17:41,760
The three of us unassisted hit the Akagi,

204
00:17:42,620 --> 00:17:47,640
whereas 10 of my planes, with 15 of the scouts and the group commander,

205
00:17:47,860 --> 00:17:50,840
something like 26 planes or so, all dropped on the Akaga.

206
00:17:51,340 --> 00:17:52,980
They caught fire and they never stopped burning.

207
00:17:53,540 --> 00:17:58,700
As I pulled out and started towards the eastern periphery of the fleet,

208
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:04,140
I found I was flying straight towards another carrier that was being torn apart by bombing.

209
00:18:04,820 --> 00:18:09,880
Despite all of their success, the Americans suffered a substantial loss of aircraft.

210
00:18:10,280 --> 00:18:13,260
Over 100 torpedo bombers were shot down.

211
00:18:14,880 --> 00:18:20,380
Armed with only a 21-inch torpedo, one forward firing and one rear-mounted machine gun,

212
00:18:20,820 --> 00:18:24,620
the antiquated plane proved extremely vulnerable to enemy attack.

213
00:18:25,780 --> 00:18:28,140
The planes of that day were very limited.

214
00:18:28,141 --> 00:18:35,420
The fighters of the Japanese were clearly superior, but the superior in speed and performance, but not in durability.

215
00:18:36,360 --> 00:18:38,640
Whereas our dive bombers were the equal of any of them.

216
00:18:39,720 --> 00:18:41,360
They carried two men.

217
00:18:41,680 --> 00:18:44,800
The rear seat man fired what we called a flexible gun.

218
00:18:45,180 --> 00:18:46,600
We had no protection below.

219
00:18:47,520 --> 00:18:53,180
The pilot has two .50 caliber guns that he fires that fire synchronized through the propellers,

220
00:18:53,560 --> 00:18:57,220
which means that you have to synchronize quite carefully so that you don't shoot your propeller off.

221
00:18:57,221 --> 00:19:03,380
As the bloody Battle of Midway came to an end, all four Japanese carriers had been destroyed.

222
00:19:04,280 --> 00:19:08,220
This was the first clearly decisive victory for the United States.

223
00:19:08,880 --> 00:19:14,340
Such a substantial loss of carrier power marked the decline of Japanese naval superiority.

224
00:19:16,840 --> 00:19:21,320
The Nimitz-class carriers are the largest warships in the world.

225
00:19:21,321 --> 00:19:24,620
at a cost of $4.5 billion each,

226
00:19:24,900 --> 00:19:28,820
today there are seven operational and two under construction.

227
00:19:29,520 --> 00:19:33,800
Aircraft carriers will return on Modern Marvels, then and now.

228
00:19:37,180 --> 00:19:39,420
In the vast Pacific Ocean,

229
00:19:39,660 --> 00:19:44,340
the Americans and the Japanese needed more than land bases to launch aircraft.

230
00:19:45,820 --> 00:19:49,980
Basically, the aircraft carriers in the war in the Pacific were mobile airfields.

231
00:19:49,981 --> 00:19:54,200
Most of the islands weren't capable of taking large numbers of aircraft,

232
00:19:54,201 --> 00:19:58,380
and really there was a large gap in the sea that needed to be filled by something.

233
00:19:59,100 --> 00:20:00,980
The aircraft carrier is an idea of tool for that.

234
00:20:02,020 --> 00:20:04,980
As the American carrier continued to prove its worth,

235
00:20:05,280 --> 00:20:10,780
the number of ocean aircraft landings increased, putting more pressure on flight deck operation.

236
00:20:11,780 --> 00:20:17,280
Factors such as pilot and crew fatigue, adverse weather conditions, and mechanical failure

237
00:20:17,281 --> 00:20:19,840
often prevented successful landings.

238
00:20:48,760 --> 00:20:49,960
The United States

239
00:20:49,961 --> 00:20:52,720
the states developed a more advanced carrier design.

240
00:20:54,300 --> 00:20:59,480
By the end of 1942, the new Essex class of U.S. carrier was ready for action.

241
00:21:00,460 --> 00:21:04,520
Essex carriers had longer and wider flight decks, more hangar space,

242
00:21:04,920 --> 00:21:07,540
and accommodations for as many as 90 aircraft.

243
00:21:08,620 --> 00:21:13,880
These carriers were fully complemented with anti-aircraft firepower fore and aft,

244
00:21:14,240 --> 00:21:16,180
including 20 5-inch guns.

245
00:21:16,181 --> 00:21:25,760
Months later, Britain and America developed another class of carrier, the smaller but slower escort carrier.

246
00:21:26,720 --> 00:21:33,300
Because their slower speeds made it more difficult for planes to launch, the Navy made use of the hydraulic catapult.

247
00:21:34,140 --> 00:21:38,240
The plane was hitched to the catapult and pressurized air was released.

248
00:21:38,241 --> 00:21:41,260
It transmitted its pressure to hydraulic fluid.

249
00:21:41,880 --> 00:21:45,240
It was then released, driving the pulleys and the aircraft forward.

250
00:21:46,180 --> 00:21:55,120
During the Second World War, the United Kingdom in particular was being strangled by the use of German submarines against the merchant convoys crossing the Atlantic.

251
00:21:55,620 --> 00:21:58,920
The range of land-based aircraft at that time was limited.

252
00:21:59,780 --> 00:22:06,840
Escort carriers, carrying only a limited number of aircraft, both for air defence and as anti-submarine weapon systems,

253
00:22:07,300 --> 00:22:12,440
were probably a key feature in the Battle of the Atlantic in the battle against the German submarines, the German U-boats.

254
00:22:13,080 --> 00:22:15,600
Without them, the Battle of the Atlantic wouldn't have been winnable.

255
00:22:16,180 --> 00:22:20,200
Meanwhile, the Americans continued to gain ground in the Pacific.

256
00:22:21,080 --> 00:22:25,240
The Japanese suffered their worst defeat of the war at the Battle of the Philippine Sea

257
00:22:25,241 --> 00:22:28,520
in what became known as the Great Mariana's Turkey Chute.

258
00:22:33,120 --> 00:22:36,660
400 Japanese planes were shot down in one day.

259
00:22:37,360 --> 00:22:43,000
Japanese naval air power was almost completely decimated as they lost three additional carriers.

260
00:22:43,001 --> 00:22:49,220
By the Battle of Leyte Gulf, the Japanese had resorted to using the kamikaze pilots.

261
00:22:54,240 --> 00:23:00,260
More than 2,500 Japanese pilots volunteered for a one-way mission to certain death

262
00:23:00,261 --> 00:23:03,780
in a desperate attempt to destroy the American carriers.

263
00:23:13,000 --> 00:23:26,280
In all, the U.S. lost a total of five carriers to Japanese bombs and kamikaze raids.

264
00:23:27,420 --> 00:23:30,820
The USS Franklin was one of the most spectacular casualties.

265
00:23:32,500 --> 00:23:39,380
The Japanese formally surrendered on the battleship USS Missouri on September 2nd, 1945.

266
00:23:40,420 --> 00:23:42,660
The war in the Pacific was predominantly an American war.

267
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:47,200
There was a need for a large number of aircraft carriers to project the American naval power.

268
00:23:47,700 --> 00:23:53,080
America was the only country which had the industrial might, the number of shipguards available, to actually build those carriers.

269
00:23:53,360 --> 00:23:57,640
So it was inevitable that America would become the predominant carrier nation.

270
00:23:58,440 --> 00:24:02,380
By the end of the war, America had deployed a massive carrier fleet.

271
00:24:02,381 --> 00:24:14,120
the jet age had arrived and adjustments had to be made in carrier design to support these powerful

272
00:24:14,121 --> 00:24:20,380
new planes naval shipyards went to work reinforcing the decks and the arresting gear of the old

273
00:24:20,381 --> 00:24:26,580
carriers the carriers were soon called back into action this time in the waters of korea

274
00:24:26,581 --> 00:24:32,880
Both the United States Navy and the Royal Navy operated carriers during the Korean War.

275
00:24:33,680 --> 00:24:39,080
The major role of these carriers was to enable airstrikes to be carried out against North Korea,

276
00:24:40,080 --> 00:24:42,680
when most of the air bases in the South were under threat.

277
00:24:44,380 --> 00:24:49,600
Carrier planes often carried out successful ground attacks against enemy troops in the field

278
00:24:49,601 --> 00:24:52,560
in order to pave the way for American soldiers.

279
00:24:52,561 --> 00:24:58,440
While they did not engage in a contest for air superiority with the MiGs,

280
00:24:58,820 --> 00:25:04,900
they did do enormously valuable work in ground attack and in addiction bombing.

281
00:25:05,960 --> 00:25:09,660
Although the carriers continued to successfully complete their missions,

282
00:25:10,280 --> 00:25:14,820
it became apparent that technical innovations in ship design were badly needed.

283
00:25:16,080 --> 00:25:21,740
It was obvious that the need to operate aircraft over a continuous basis

284
00:25:21,741 --> 00:25:24,600
to recover damaged aircraft on board the ship.

285
00:25:25,100 --> 00:25:26,960
And basically to speed up the whole operation

286
00:25:26,961 --> 00:25:29,520
would require something more than the axial deck.

287
00:25:30,220 --> 00:25:34,500
In the axial deck, an aircraft flew directly down the length of the axis of the deck.

288
00:25:34,920 --> 00:25:39,860
And often, if it missed the arrestor wires, which strung across the deck,

289
00:25:39,861 --> 00:25:44,980
then it would fly directly down the deck into aircraft parked at the other end.

290
00:25:45,740 --> 00:25:50,420
So in order to mitigate against that, steel barriers were put up, steel cables and ropes.

291
00:25:51,040 --> 00:25:53,940
This, of course, usually meant the loss of the aircraft and probably the pilot.

292
00:25:55,100 --> 00:26:02,200
On August 29, 1965, the USS Lake Champlain recovered astronauts Gordon Cooper and Charles Conrad

293
00:26:02,201 --> 00:26:05,000
after their record-breaking eight-day space flight.

294
00:26:05,700 --> 00:26:08,980
Modern marvels then and now will continue in a moment.

295
00:26:12,400 --> 00:26:16,720
Exhaustive research went into finding a solution to safe deck operations,

296
00:26:17,240 --> 00:26:19,920
and Britain came up with several significant developments.

297
00:26:20,420 --> 00:26:23,400
The United Kingdom developed the angled flight deck.

298
00:26:23,780 --> 00:26:30,400
That would allow aircraft to land at an angle of 2.5, 5, 7.5 degrees off the centre line of the ship.

299
00:26:30,900 --> 00:26:33,640
It would allow aircraft to park at the bow of the ship.

300
00:26:34,020 --> 00:26:39,440
And if the aircraft missed the arrest of wires as it was landing, it could just take off again and go round into another circuit.

301
00:26:39,880 --> 00:26:44,120
It wasn't really put into proper use by the Royal Navy until the Americans had done so.

302
00:26:44,360 --> 00:26:48,100
The US Navy watched the angled deck experiments with some interest.

303
00:26:48,101 --> 00:26:51,820
and they actually produced the first aircraft carrier with an angled deck.

304
00:26:52,860 --> 00:26:56,020
Britton also developed the mirrored landing site.

305
00:26:57,060 --> 00:27:02,060
These enabled pilots to be able to land their aircraft in most weather conditions,

306
00:27:02,440 --> 00:27:07,220
in day or night, using an aid on the deck of the ship, rather than a Batman.

307
00:27:07,800 --> 00:27:13,680
He could watch himself coming into land against a natural reference point, usually some lights.

308
00:27:14,620 --> 00:27:20,000
New innovations had been made in landing, but the jets also had problems taking off.

309
00:27:20,760 --> 00:27:26,460
The pre-jet aircraft were lighter and had a greater wingspan, enabling them to take off on their own power.

310
00:27:27,640 --> 00:27:29,620
The jets needed additional help.

311
00:27:31,220 --> 00:27:38,300
As aircraft became heavier and heavier, requiring more fuel, requiring heavier war lads to be carried,

312
00:27:38,301 --> 00:27:43,580
they needed something more than a hydraulic system to launch them from the deck.

313
00:27:43,680 --> 00:27:48,660
The British engineers got together and developed a steam catapult jet aircraft.

314
00:27:48,800 --> 00:27:52,100
It's connected to the steam catapult by a series of steel bridles,

315
00:27:52,380 --> 00:27:54,160
which go to a runner in the deck.

316
00:27:54,680 --> 00:27:58,540
That runner in the deck is connected into basically a cylinder,

317
00:27:58,720 --> 00:27:59,960
which is built into the flight deck.

318
00:28:00,380 --> 00:28:02,980
Steam pressure is built up, the pressure released,

319
00:28:04,000 --> 00:28:06,680
the dolly, as it's called, travels down the flight deck,

320
00:28:06,780 --> 00:28:09,240
dragging the aircraft with it, and flings it into the air.

321
00:28:09,241 --> 00:28:15,680
The first class of carrier designed to launch jet aircraft was the Forrestal class.

322
00:28:16,380 --> 00:28:19,040
The Forrestal was the biggest carrier ever built.

323
00:28:19,800 --> 00:28:22,760
The flight deck measured over 1,000 feet in length.

324
00:28:23,360 --> 00:28:29,060
It had strengthened flight decks and larger deck lifts to take the aircraft from the deck into the hangar below.

325
00:28:30,100 --> 00:28:37,920
Its four five-bladed propellers could generate 200,000 horsepower, giving the vessel a top speed of 30 knots.

326
00:28:38,920 --> 00:28:41,620
Designers incorporated all the new innovations,

327
00:28:42,160 --> 00:28:45,500
steam catapults, angled deck, and better arrestor gear.

328
00:28:46,900 --> 00:28:50,840
Even though Britain's carriers had served successfully in the war,

329
00:28:51,380 --> 00:28:55,880
the Royal Navy found they could no longer afford to build massive carriers.

330
00:28:56,400 --> 00:28:57,760
They always had less money to spend.

331
00:28:58,480 --> 00:29:02,920
Right down the line, a lot of the major innovations that made a supercarrier work

332
00:29:02,921 --> 00:29:07,267
were developed by the British and then adopted by the U.S.

333
00:29:07,268 --> 00:29:09,440
Navy in a much greater scale.

334
00:29:10,120 --> 00:29:12,720
The British introduced them, the Americans adopted them,

335
00:29:12,960 --> 00:29:17,580
and then built a ship that could really use them to a much higher degree of efficiency,

336
00:29:17,800 --> 00:29:19,740
and that was the Forrest All-Supercarrier class.

337
00:29:20,400 --> 00:29:24,640
In the mid-1960s, the British government decided, for political and economic reasons,

338
00:29:24,641 --> 00:29:27,180
it could no longer develop the conventional aircraft carrier,

339
00:29:27,480 --> 00:29:31,340
capable of taking fixed-wing jets and operating them at sea for long periods.

340
00:29:32,220 --> 00:29:36,900
So the Royal Navy developed a smaller light support aircraft carrier,

341
00:29:37,600 --> 00:29:39,460
which came to be known as the Invincible class.

342
00:29:40,360 --> 00:29:45,020
Basically, these are aircraft carriers capable of carrying the Sea Harrier jump jet and helicopters.

343
00:29:45,520 --> 00:29:49,060
The primary role is to defend a task group against air attack

344
00:29:49,061 --> 00:29:53,360
and to be used to carry large numbers of anti-submarine warfare helicopters

345
00:29:53,361 --> 00:29:56,420
in order to keep submarines away from valued targets.

346
00:29:57,220 --> 00:30:00,660
While Britain was cutting down on the size of her carriers,

347
00:30:00,661 --> 00:30:04,300
America was again making them larger and more powerful.

348
00:30:05,340 --> 00:30:07,680
As carrier development continued to progress,

349
00:30:08,080 --> 00:30:11,520
naval aviators soon found themselves fighting another war,

350
00:30:11,940 --> 00:30:13,780
this time in Southeast Asia.

351
00:30:14,520 --> 00:30:17,560
From the very beginning of America's involvement in Vietnam,

352
00:30:18,500 --> 00:30:20,440
the aircraft carrier was an important platform.

353
00:30:21,160 --> 00:30:22,700
It provided a mobile airfield,

354
00:30:23,280 --> 00:30:28,220
which enabled strikes to be mounted against targets in North Vietnam like Hanoi and Haiphong.

355
00:30:28,221 --> 00:30:31,440
without having to use bases in the south

356
00:30:31,441 --> 00:30:33,760
which were vulnerable to terrorist attack

357
00:30:33,761 --> 00:30:38,300
and which in any case were already overcrowded with Air Force and Army aircraft.

358
00:30:41,460 --> 00:30:45,080
The Navy pilots observed during the Vietnamese War,

359
00:30:45,160 --> 00:30:46,480
just as the Air Force pilots did,

360
00:30:47,000 --> 00:30:50,180
that the training they had received had not been rigorous enough.

361
00:30:50,620 --> 00:30:54,340
The training in the previous nine years had been too concerned with safety,

362
00:30:54,341 --> 00:30:56,080
and if you're going to be very concerned about safety,

363
00:30:56,081 --> 00:30:57,800
you're not going to press the loneliness of the envelope.

364
00:30:58,160 --> 00:31:03,560
The Navy demanded of itself better training and created the top gun school.

365
00:31:04,420 --> 00:31:10,620
This new breed of highly trained aviator was much more capable than the pilots of just a few years before.

366
00:31:11,320 --> 00:31:17,520
And a major innovation in the way in which carriers are fueled would completely revolutionize the present day carrier.

367
00:31:18,540 --> 00:31:24,180
One of the biggest strides in carrier development came with the advent of the nuclear powered carrier.

368
00:31:24,820 --> 00:31:27,420
Its size and capabilities are astounding.

369
00:31:28,200 --> 00:31:32,240
A total of 17 stories from superstructure to engine room.

370
00:31:33,780 --> 00:31:38,360
When the Americans developed a nuclear reactor that was economic and safe enough to go to sea,

371
00:31:38,361 --> 00:31:42,320
it was obvious that they would put it in their capital ships, the aircraft carriers.

372
00:31:43,800 --> 00:31:50,240
In 1961, the USS Enterprise became the world's first nuclear-powered aircraft carrier.

373
00:31:50,241 --> 00:31:54,160
propelled by eight atomic reactors

374
00:31:54,161 --> 00:31:56,520
this alternative source of energy

375
00:31:56,521 --> 00:31:59,320
enabled the ship to operate for 12 years

376
00:31:59,321 --> 00:32:00,760
without refueling

377
00:32:00,761 --> 00:32:05,960
nuclear propulsion made it a different ship

378
00:32:05,961 --> 00:32:07,980
because now you can operate this thing

379
00:32:07,981 --> 00:32:10,080
for 800,000 miles

380
00:32:10,081 --> 00:32:12,400
in the latest ships without having to refuel

381
00:32:12,401 --> 00:32:17,120
it was over 10 years

382
00:32:17,121 --> 00:32:20,000
until another nuclear powered carrier was launched

383
00:32:20,860 --> 00:32:23,860
Built by Newport News Shipbuilding in 1974,

384
00:32:24,760 --> 00:32:28,420
the USS Nimitz became the second nuclear-propelled carrier.

385
00:32:30,420 --> 00:32:34,780
Unlike the Enterprise, this ship relied on only two atomic reactors.

386
00:32:35,720 --> 00:32:41,140
This reduction in reactor space provided badly needed storage space in the decks below.

387
00:32:42,600 --> 00:32:46,780
Below the flight deck of a modern aircraft carrier, there's virtually a complete city.

388
00:32:46,781 --> 00:32:52,140
It has everything from barbershops to aircraft workshops, bakeries.

389
00:32:52,520 --> 00:32:54,420
It has facilities for sleeping, for eating.

390
00:32:54,940 --> 00:33:01,780
It gives it a flavor of being a city like any in mainland USA or in continental Europe.

391
00:33:05,140 --> 00:33:11,080
The rest of the aircraft carrier is designed to get from point A to point B and to keep those aircraft in the air,

392
00:33:11,081 --> 00:33:16,660
which means there's a great deal of area that is open for machines and it's open for fuel.

393
00:33:17,220 --> 00:33:20,100
Remember, you can put that aircraft carrier at sea and leave it at sea for six months.

394
00:33:20,540 --> 00:33:25,360
Everything that can be fixed on an aircraft and then go wrong with an aircraft is done on that aircraft carrier,

395
00:33:25,480 --> 00:33:27,700
from changing an engine to almost taking a wing off.

396
00:33:28,500 --> 00:33:33,340
The prime role of a modern aircraft carrier is to keep aircraft at sea for a prolonged period

397
00:33:33,341 --> 00:33:38,280
to enable air operations against other ships and submarines or against land targets.

398
00:33:38,281 --> 00:33:44,320
Every effort is made to keep this small city independent and mobile.

399
00:33:45,160 --> 00:33:48,980
Any replenishment the carrier may need is brought to it from the supply ship.

400
00:33:50,060 --> 00:33:54,720
Everything from food and fuel to additional crew members.

401
00:34:00,140 --> 00:34:04,500
Meatball is a series of lights that aid the pilot in lining up for the landing.

402
00:34:04,880 --> 00:34:06,620
These lights are always on.

403
00:34:07,260 --> 00:34:12,080
Aircraft carriers will continue in a moment on Modern Marvel's Then and Now.

404
00:34:15,820 --> 00:34:21,400
The flight deck layout of a modern carrier is a direct reflection of operating procedures.

405
00:34:22,340 --> 00:34:29,660
There are four deck edge elevators, one on the port side where it can bring aircraft to the pair of steam catapults in the rear of the ship.

406
00:34:30,320 --> 00:34:34,000
The others on the forward and aft of the island on the starboard side.

407
00:34:34,001 --> 00:34:39,640
The positioning of aircraft in relation to the elevators is crucial

408
00:34:39,641 --> 00:34:44,560
because rapid flow of aircraft is essential when a large airstrike is launched.

409
00:34:46,020 --> 00:34:51,180
The carrier catapults are all at slightly different angles to allow multiple launches.

410
00:34:52,040 --> 00:34:56,940
The carrier can launch its entire complement of 90 aircraft in under 30 minutes.

411
00:34:57,620 --> 00:35:01,100
The flight deck crew is the vital key to this operation.

412
00:35:01,101 --> 00:35:11,100
The carrier flight deck is probably as close to a ballet choreographed industrial situation that you'll ever see.

413
00:35:11,460 --> 00:35:17,660
Each man on the carrier deck has to move in a certain way at a certain time or his life is at stake.

414
00:35:17,661 --> 00:35:25,040
And he has to do it while he's doing something important, arming bombs, making sure that the airplane is properly inspected, positioning it.

415
00:35:25,280 --> 00:35:27,140
It's a remarkable engineering accomplishment.

416
00:35:27,141 --> 00:35:32,040
The color of the shirt tells the function of the man.

417
00:35:33,140 --> 00:35:36,380
Yellow shirts direct all the airplanes on the flight deck.

418
00:35:36,900 --> 00:35:38,840
Nothing moves without their permission.

419
00:35:40,100 --> 00:35:44,080
White shirts inspect the aircraft as they approach the catapult for launch.

420
00:35:45,300 --> 00:35:48,560
Green shirts hook the planes up and perform all maintenance.

421
00:35:49,720 --> 00:35:51,960
Purple shirts fuel the aircraft.

422
00:35:52,720 --> 00:35:54,500
Blue shirts chalk and chain them.

423
00:35:55,060 --> 00:35:56,580
Red shirts handle ordnance.

424
00:35:57,140 --> 00:35:59,573
Silver suits are the firefighters.

425
00:35:59,574 --> 00:36:03,580
If an airplane crashes, their job is to rescue the crew.

426
00:36:04,440 --> 00:36:09,080
The brown shirts are the plane captains, responsible for the care of each airplane.

427
00:36:10,080 --> 00:36:16,800
They work long hours under arduous conditions, and although they are well trained, the work is inherently dangerous.

428
00:36:17,980 --> 00:36:22,480
On the flight deck, one mistake has the potential for lethal consequences.

429
00:36:22,481 --> 00:36:29,480
The extreme proximity of man and machine is a fundamental hazard.

430
00:36:29,481 --> 00:36:36,480
The planes are congested in close quarters on the flight and hangar deck.

431
00:36:36,481 --> 00:36:41,480
Every measure is taken to compact planes and other equipment when not in use.

432
00:36:45,480 --> 00:36:49,480
The planes have wings that fold up to reduce their size.

433
00:36:49,481 --> 00:36:55,580
When the plane lands, almost immediately it's taken and parked on the deck out of the way of flight deck operations.

434
00:36:57,460 --> 00:37:05,640
Built into the flight deck are four powerful steam catapults that induced acceleration more than five times the force of gravity.

435
00:37:06,400 --> 00:37:10,400
Within two and a half, three seconds, you go from zero to 150, 160 miles an hour,

436
00:37:10,480 --> 00:37:12,580
and it's just really a trip 60 feet off the water.

437
00:37:12,581 --> 00:37:14,680
There's nothing that can compare to it, absolutely nothing.

438
00:37:14,681 --> 00:37:27,013
It's an extraordinary thing to see in an operation and to see the fantastic amounts of energy that are required to loft these big airplanes, you know, 25,000, 30,000

439
00:37:27,014 --> 00:37:33,740
pound airplanes off the carrier deck from zero speed to flying speed and perhaps 120 feet.

440
00:37:34,500 --> 00:37:41,280
Putting 20 tons of airplane on a patch of rolling carrier deck can make even the most experienced pilot nervous.

441
00:37:42,040 --> 00:37:45,420
He watches the landing aid system for glide path corrections.

442
00:37:45,920 --> 00:37:47,240
He never watches the deck.

443
00:37:48,380 --> 00:37:54,560
A 50-ton pull on the tail hook brings the airplane to a complete stop in two seconds.

444
00:37:57,680 --> 00:38:01,820
By the time you have realized that you're about to land an aircraft carrier, you're on the deck.

445
00:38:02,280 --> 00:38:03,320
It happens that fast.

446
00:38:03,321 --> 00:38:12,700
When the pilot lands, his plane is at full speed.

447
00:38:13,300 --> 00:38:16,860
He has only a split second to determine whether he has caught the wire

448
00:38:16,861 --> 00:38:20,200
or must continue at full throttle for another approach.

449
00:38:23,320 --> 00:38:26,600
Night landings constitute an even greater danger.

450
00:38:27,400 --> 00:38:32,280
From 10 miles out, the flight is directed from the carrier air traffic control center.

451
00:38:33,320 --> 00:38:38,520
A night carrier landing, your margin for error is down to zip.

452
00:38:38,840 --> 00:38:39,860
